  "caveat": "Lexical-anchor co-occurrence is a PROXY for evidential sufficiency and errs in BOTH directions. It OVERCOUNTS cross-window mass: a sentence whose anchors are scattered may still be fully verifiable from one window (repeated entities, anchors that are incidental surface tokens rather than load-bearing evidence, or a single window that entails the claim without containing every surface form). It UNDERCOUNTS: paraphrased or inferentially supported evidence carries no lexical match at all, so genuinely composed multi-hop sentences whose support is paraphrased are scored as within-window (or dropped as unanchored). The census bounds where cross-window information COULD live; it does not measure how much of it is needed.",
  "gpu_note": "G0 completed on CPU in ~40 s wall (10 arena subsets + 2,752-row gold) before the author amendment on GPU use arrived; no scanning work remained to port. The G1 embedding sweep - the only genuinely heavy step - ran on GPU0 and GPU2 in parallel, GPU1 untouched.",
  "structural_finding": "Dispersion is overwhelmingly CROSS-CHUNK, not long-range within one document: cross-chunk share equals cross-window share exactly on 6 of 10 subsets, and where a single chunk does hold every anchor the covering span is short (median 74-368 chars, p90 under 1,000 on 8 of 11 groups). Only techqa (10.2%), expertqa (5.2%) and finqa (4.3%) carry any within-chunk span beyond 1,500 chars. The adjacent-window neighbour channel is therefore close to empty; the composable information that exists sits across separate retrieved documents, which the window-max already spans."
